WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.401 --> 00:00:06.770
JEFF KEMBER: My
name is Jeff Kember.

00:00:06.770 --> 00:00:09.900
We're going to be talking about
high performance file storage.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.070
I've been at Google about
2 and 1/2 years now.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.050
I'm a technical director for
media in the Office of the CTO.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.800
So cloud storage it's a
really complicated thing

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:21.774
for some people.

00:00:21.774 --> 00:00:23.440
When they come and
talk with us about it

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.450
they're like, wait there's
a couple different options.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:26.408
How does all this work?

00:00:26.408 --> 00:00:29.020
So Google has a ton of
compute, and they also

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.040
have a lot of storage.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.920
And they try and keep it
really separated into just

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.420
two simple areas.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.340
The Google Cloud
Storage that you'll

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.220
be running on when you run
a Google Cloud platform

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.809
is the same technology that
Google runs all the rest

00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:42.850
of its infrastructure on.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.599
I think that's kind
of the important thing

00:00:44.599 --> 00:00:47.104
is that we don't have a
separate version for Enterprise

00:00:47.104 --> 00:00:48.520
and a separate
version for Google.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:53.020
We just have the one version
that is Google Scale.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.110
So oftentimes we talk
about object store.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.690
If you want to bring many,
many hundreds of petabytes

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.720
or exabytes to us,
object store is obviously

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:02.811
where you want to put it.

00:01:02.811 --> 00:01:04.269
There's other
interesting use cases

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.070
though that take advantage
of our persistent disk,

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:10.180
and we have a hard drive variant
as well as an SSD variant.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.320
You'll find it's called
just PD for short or PDSSD,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.280
to make the distinction
for ultra low latency.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.270
We also have local SSD.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:21.250
So the distinction
here is persistent disk

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.910
is attached, sorry, it's
network attached I should say,

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:27.730
and striped and can
be up to 64 terabytes,

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:29.770
and expect that to grow.

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.080
Local SSD is up to
three terabytes,

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.520
and that's physically
attached to the machine.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:38.542
In terms of our object
store, the cool thing

00:01:38.542 --> 00:01:40.500
is we have a couple of
different flavors of it.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.140
So multi-regional
allows you to be

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.540
able to store data
in two locations that

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.060
are more than 150 miles apart.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.640
And that's compelling in the
fact that Google has a private

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.860
network-- we'll talk about
it a little bit later--

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.792
as well as we have dedicated
lines under the oceans

00:01:55.792 --> 00:01:57.000
and between the data centers.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.420
So multi-regional is really
fantastic for content serving.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.090
Regional is
interesting in the fact

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.810
that, for the
highest performance,

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.800
the data is stored
in the data center

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.960
that you're performing
the compute operations on.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.060
It's also slightly
more economical.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.210
Nearline is our storage
for data that you

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:17.730
want to access sometime
in the next year.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.730
And if the data's going to
sit as in an LTO replacement,

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:24.270
then coldline is the
appropriate option for that.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.220
So the cool thing
with multi-regional

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.860
is the fact that it is
priced at the same price

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.860
point as our previous
version, except you

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.890
get the multi-regional aspect
included in that price.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.670
So if you want to do content
serving and use either RC, CDN,

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:42.030
or use Akamai, or
use Fastly then

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.352
you're serving from
multiple locations.

00:02:44.352 --> 00:02:45.810
So you're going to
be in a scenario

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.690
where your latency
is reduced further.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.130
Simple API, these
are commands that

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.750
are going to allow you to
create a bucket, either

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.470
a nearline bucket or
a regional bucket,

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.900
and then you can read an
image from either of those.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.320
It's the same API across
all of our storage,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.869
and we're really
proud of the fact

00:03:05.869 --> 00:03:08.160
that you don't have to be
using different systems to be

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:08.970
able to access it.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.480
In terms of pricing,
as I mentioned,

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.440
it's really inexpensive to
use multi-regional as well

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.330
as regional in terms
of reads and writes.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:23.570
We've priced it so that
you can almost make--

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.810
you're able to do
production level reads

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:28.740
with it without having
to pay additional fees.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.320
You can see it's $0.04
per 10,000 operations.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.150
So it's highly
performant, and you

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.490
can see that we have
11 nines of durability.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.530
That level of durability
is consistent across

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:41.760
our entire storage offering.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.250
So for regional, for many media
customers that I work with,

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:48.680
regional is sufficient if
they're not serving content.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.276
It's optimal in
the fact that you

00:03:50.276 --> 00:03:52.400
know you're committing a
right to that data center.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.810
You don't have to
wait for two rights

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.434
to occur in two
different data centers,

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:58.490
it all just happens right
there within that zone

00:03:58.490 --> 00:03:59.940
and within that region.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:04.130
So it's highly performant,
and at the same time you only

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:05.550
pay for it as you use it.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.340
So nearline was brought out
as our original LTO tape

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:15.702
replacement, and you
can even serve content

00:04:15.702 --> 00:04:18.160
from nearline or coldline,
which is interesting in the fact

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.840
that it's the same couple
milliseconds at most, typically

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:26.380
less than that, access time
for any of these platforms.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:29.470
With nearline, you can
work out the pricing such

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:33.010
that if you're going to read
the data in less than a year,

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:34.420
nearline makes sense.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.580
We have a tool that
automatically ages the data

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:38.950
off, so that if you
wish to enable it,

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:41.399
you can take the
data from regional

00:04:41.399 --> 00:04:42.940
and then move it to
nearline and then

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:47.130
move it to coldline
over time automatically.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.810
From a performance
standpoint, we

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.450
came up with an SLA that was
several seconds of access time.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:53.790
And this is in contrast
to previous offerings

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.350
that from other
vendors that may have

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:56.840
been several hours
in access time,

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.680
requiring a different API.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.740
So the opportunity to have
nearline available to you

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:06.090
in a millisecond capacity we're
really excited about that.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:09.240
And that enabled the opportunity
to have coldline available.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.510
And for us, we
typically advise people

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:15.270
if you're not going to read
the data in a year put it

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.470
on coldline.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.920
Both nearline and coldline
have a one month, a 30 day

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.120
requirement, in terms of cost.

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:27.400
Multi-regional and regional
are just as you use them.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.176
Again, consistent
API, you can see

00:05:29.176 --> 00:05:30.550
that we're using
the same GET/PUT

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.650
commands that you saw earlier.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:36.100
And that comment about
serving the data,

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.832
performance wise
it'll do that, cost

00:05:37.832 --> 00:05:39.790
wise we feel it's a better
option for you to be

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.800
able to move it to
another tier, which you

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.180
can do on a per object basis.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.570
So this is a little bit
more of a breakdown here.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.540
You can see on the
left hand side there

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:51.280
is no minimum storage duration.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.830
On the right hand side, we
do do a 30 and then a 90 day.

00:05:53.830 --> 00:05:56.200
So it's really intended
to be more for,

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.660
as you move toward the
right, more for longer term.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.400
We have quite a
few customers who

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.060
are contractually obligated
to store three copies of data.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.760
And in that context
they've now started

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.370
moving to storing two
copies of LTO tape

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:11.200
and one on Google Cloud.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.070
And a number of them have now
gone to one copy of LTO tape

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:15.580
and cloud.

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:16.480
And they're finding
that they're not

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.490
restoring from tape
on prem, the tapes

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.375
are going off for
off-site storage.

00:06:20.375 --> 00:06:22.000
And they're restoring
from Google Cloud

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.833
because they can get
it back in milliseconds

00:06:23.833 --> 00:06:25.690
and only restore the
objects they need,

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:29.050
as opposed to restoring online
an entire LTO tape which takes

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.240
time and the robot and such.

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.950
So if you want to
use them all together

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:36.020
the cool part, as I
mentioned, you can do it--

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.030
we announced this when
we announced coldline,

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.779
but we didn't really
get a lot of fanfare,

00:06:39.779 --> 00:06:42.560
the idea of having
the ability to have

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.650
multiple objects in a bucket
and being able to control which

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:47.959
category each object is in.

00:06:47.959 --> 00:06:50.000
And that really helps from
a cost and performance

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.420
standpoint, because you're in
a scenario where you can say,

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.080
I have this 17 petabyte
archive and 50 terabytes

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.650
of it I'm going to be using.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:01.250
So I'd like to have
that available to me

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:04.804
at a higher and easier
read cost, let's say.

00:07:04.804 --> 00:07:06.470
And you put that in
a regional, and then

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:07.910
you can have another
portion of that available

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.326
in multi-regional, because
that's content that's finished,

00:07:10.326 --> 00:07:13.172
final and you want to
serve that content.

00:07:13.172 --> 00:07:15.380
This also mentions the data
lifecycle and the ability

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:19.890
to move easily between
in an automated fashion.

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.740
So when moving to
block storage, we

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.940
have persistent disk,
which I mentioned earlier.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:27.320
So the machines boot,
when you fire up the VM,

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.400
you get persistent HDD so just
standard PD, and it performs

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:33.590
while it's our boot disk.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:34.940
Quite a few customers use that.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:37.700
It's inexpensive,
network attached.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:42.140
The opportunity to move to
SSD for PD is also compelling.

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:43.670
We have a single
mode filer, which

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:45.560
I'll talk about in
the few minutes that's

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:50.090
backed by both local SSD,
essentially as RAM replacement,

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:52.550
in addition to on-board
RAM, but also it

00:07:52.550 --> 00:07:56.960
has persistent SSD on it
for gain, high performance.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:58.910
We're looking at 64
terabytes of gain,

00:07:58.910 --> 00:08:02.990
but we can increase
that on the PD.

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:06.980
So local SSD, these are
three different use cases

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:08.780
that I brought up as examples.

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:12.142
So Avere is a on
premise, physical box,

00:08:12.142 --> 00:08:13.850
and that's what people
are most used to .

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:17.900
They also have a cloud variant,
which just called the vFXT,

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:20.750
and it's quite a bit different
than the on premise box.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.720
What's interesting about it
is it's the same algorithm,

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:25.580
but it's highly scalable.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.820
It's your cash and your
file system together.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:31.359
And the advantage of being
able to run that on the cloud

00:08:31.359 --> 00:08:32.900
is you start with
three nodes and you

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.064
can scale up to 30 or 50.

00:08:35.064 --> 00:08:37.730
You get the high throughput that
we have our machines, currently

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:41.210
16 gig per VM on a
40 gig interconnect,

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.460
and that's all software
defined networking.

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:46.430
So we're able to maintain that
line rate as you scale out.

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:49.490
With the vFXT, you can run it
on PD, SSD, as I mentioned,

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:52.040
or if you wish you can
run it on a local SSD.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:53.840
When you run it on
local SSD you get

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.220
a couple cool things happening.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:57.470
2X performance across
the board increase.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:09:01.220
And for random reads, which for
many media customers or people

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:05.540
doing high computational loads,
it's a lot of random reads.

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:07.340
You get a 4 1/2 times
speed improvement,

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:09.881
because, there's no network I/O
required to go to the network

00:09:09.881 --> 00:09:11.480
attached storage
to get your data.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.630
It's just sitting there,
all ready, ready to go.

00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:16.310
For transcoding,
I have customers

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:19.020
who will go use
FFmpeg, grab the data,

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.645
put it on a machine
with local SSD,

00:09:21.645 --> 00:09:24.200
have the mezzanine file
there, do a transcode

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.550
to 27 different flavors of
codecs and resolutions and bit

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:30.399
rates, spit them all
out onto a local SSD,

00:09:30.399 --> 00:09:31.940
and then copy it
over when it's done.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:33.815
They don't even have to
set up a file system.

00:09:33.815 --> 00:09:36.530
So that's, I think, a
highly useful use case.

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:38.750
It's also available
in small chunks,

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:40.820
375 up to three terabytes.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.380
For simulation,
which Adrian is going

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:44.637
to chat about in a
couple of minutes here,

00:09:44.637 --> 00:09:46.220
the simulation is
exciting in the fact

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:49.700
that you can do fluid,
just a fluid simulation.

00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:51.980
You can do destruction work.

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:54.320
In the same simulation
idea, you can also

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:58.850
do finite element analysis
with car crashing simulation,

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.310
for instance, using our
GPUs, if you choose.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:03.560
All of this can make
use of our local SSD

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:06.140
for ultra high speed
storage, and you

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:10.150
can supplement that with a
cloud file system as well.

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:14.470
This is a slightly
complicated and barely legible

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:18.201
graph it looks like
of hybrid rendering.

00:10:18.201 --> 00:10:20.200
What we have on the left
hand side of the screen

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:24.110
is an on premises display,
and on the right hand side,

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:24.965
we have the cloud.

00:10:24.965 --> 00:10:26.590
So really what we're
talking about here

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:30.090
is there's a file server,
there's asset management,

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:31.426
there's licensing.

00:10:31.426 --> 00:10:32.800
There's all the
normal things you

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.329
would need to be able to make
a picture in this diagram.

00:10:35.329 --> 00:10:37.870
On the right hand side, we have
the cloud equivalent of that.

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:40.540
So if you wish, you can
point back on prem across

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:44.560
and interconnect VPN however
you wish to configure with us.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.680
Then you can point back
on prem to get access

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.650
to your asset management and
your cloud license server.

00:10:50.650 --> 00:10:52.420
What's interesting
about this, for me, is

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:54.640
if you remove the
license server component,

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:58.420
and you change out the
compute VMs as being

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:01.060
standard compute VMs,
and change them for GPUs,

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:04.000
I would change the word
rendering to oil and gas.

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.510
Or if you decided
that you wanted

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:10.440
to run a very large
amount of compute,

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.370
like 100,000 course
for instance,

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:14.560
and you wanted to do
financial simulation work,

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.700
I would change the word
oil and gas to financial.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.091
The framework looks the same.

00:11:19.091 --> 00:11:19.840
It doesn't change.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:24.010
We have genomics customers who
are using this same framework,

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:26.560
and they're able to complete
high performance compute

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:30.530
workloads on a permanent
basis on our platform.

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:32.361
So in terms of
customer use cases,

00:11:32.361 --> 00:11:33.860
in an Enterprise
event we want to be

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.410
able to tell people, hey, look,
we have customers using this.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:37.180
Here are the customers.

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:38.690
So we're excited
about the product,

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:40.648
and here are the customers
who are experiencing

00:11:40.648 --> 00:11:42.620
great success with it.

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:44.720
Most of these customers
have come to us and said,

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:47.934
we don't want to be in
the business of having--

00:11:47.934 --> 00:11:49.600
we don't want to run
data farms anymore.

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:51.891
We don't want to have to have
a huge amount of storage.

00:11:51.891 --> 00:11:54.360
We wish to supplement the high
speed disks we have locally,

00:11:54.360 --> 00:11:57.180
but the Tier 2, some customers
have an object store.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.100
The customers who have
a large tape robot

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.766
they want to be in a
scenario where they

00:12:00.766 --> 00:12:02.650
can start to move off of that.

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:04.920
As far as ecosystems
go, if you have

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.080
any of these products
on premises currently,

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:09.390
you're in a scenario where
these are all cloud enabled

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:11.310
to varying degrees.

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:14.370
These customers, sorry,
these partners I should say,

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:17.070
form our ecosystem
that allows you

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.800
to be able to extend your
current storage directly

00:12:19.800 --> 00:12:23.860
onto Google Cloud in a
fully managed fashion.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.450
And in terms of partners,
I mentioned Avere earlier.

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:30.215
We also partner with
PixCache out of the UK.

00:12:30.215 --> 00:12:32.480
NetApp is listed on here.

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:34.810
These are, again,
fantastic opportunities

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:37.670
to be able to take
brands that you know,

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:40.090
love, and use currently,
and be able to just extend

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:42.740
the product that you have.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:44.300
In terms of our
backbone, you can

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:46.970
see we have a whole lot
of lines on this graph.

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:51.170
The opportunity to expand
this, it's in 2018.

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:53.690
We're adding additional
under-sea links.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:56.000
You'll notice there's
multiple links as well.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:58.400
I meet customers who
are under the impression

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:00.560
from our marketing
information that we only

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:02.700
have kind of single
links under the oceans.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.780
We have multiple
paths, and we're

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:06.530
the only cloud provider
on the planet that

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:08.490
has a private global network.

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:11.540
So in this depiction you
can jump on in Helsinki,

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:15.740
and then jump off in Taiwan, or
in New York, or back in Tokyo,

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:17.005
or Sydney.

00:13:17.005 --> 00:13:19.130
And in that scenario, you're
on our private network

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:20.030
the entire time.

00:13:20.030 --> 00:13:21.950
So it's all software
defined networking.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:23.870
We have our own
quality of service.

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:26.690
We're in a scenario where it's
encrypted in motion and at rest

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:28.220
on our storage.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:31.790
From a peering standpoint, we
have over 100 peering sites.

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:35.120
So if you're with
Equinix or other

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:39.200
there's a high likelihood that
you can run fiber directly

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:41.750
into our switch and have
a direct connect with us.

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:44.810
Talk with us about
those opportunities.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:47.330
We have a roadmap, we're
offering new opportunities

00:13:47.330 --> 00:13:48.680
in that area over time.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:53.060
But the opportunity to be able
to be near one of our pops,

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:55.220
pretty much in most major
cities on the planet

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:57.350
where there's fiber
peering, we're

00:13:57.350 --> 00:14:00.180
already in that [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:02.460
From a machine
learning standpoint,

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.820
this is an area that we're
really excited about in terms

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:09.480
of what can this do
for financial, media

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:11.790
customers, retail?

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.770
What's exciting for me
about machine learning

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:16.860
is the fact that it's prevalent
across our entire product line,

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:19.830
and Google has gone to the
trouble of building TPUs, which

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:22.000
are TensorFlow processing units.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:26.300
So that gives us the ability
to run on custom hardware

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:27.650
at extremely high speeds.

00:14:27.650 --> 00:14:30.770
That was the engine that
was powering the AlphaGo

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:34.730
competition that
Google won last year.

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:36.620
What this means to you,
though, is normally

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:38.750
you'd do your
training on CPU or GPU

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:41.210
and then you'd deploy
on that; however,

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:44.480
with TensorFlow units you can
get another order of magnitude,

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:46.460
typically faster processing.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:50.090
And you can see we've
announced Cloud ML Engine.

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:52.770
We can talk more about
that in other sessions.

00:14:52.770 --> 00:14:54.320
But you can see
we have an option

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.630
to go with open
source TensorFlow,

00:14:56.630 --> 00:14:58.400
and you can run
that on any cloud

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.761
you wish, on your local
laptop or a data center.

00:15:00.761 --> 00:15:02.510
You can take those
same graphs you've made

00:15:02.510 --> 00:15:04.040
and also run them on Google.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:05.660
So like our
container engine it's

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:10.190
a highly portable, open source,
machine learning technology.

00:15:10.190 --> 00:15:15.320
The ML APIs are TensorFlow but
with our models already trained

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:16.760
and ready to run on them.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:20.150
The cool part with that is
that you get our 100 languages

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:23.530
and translate, you get
our video and now vision,

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:25.850
I guess I should clarify,
vision and now video API.

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:29.780
We announced the
video API yesterday

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:31.280
as general availability.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:33.650
And the exciting part
about that is it's

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:35.060
the Vision API on wheels.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:37.250
So you can feed it
large movie clips,

00:15:37.250 --> 00:15:40.880
it'll generate metadata for you,
and it'll do logo detection.

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:44.000
And it will allow you to do
optical character reading,

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:46.730
and it will tell you
where in the world

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:50.300
the scene is if it can
identify landmarks and such.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.310
I urge people, if they
want to play with this,

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:54.290
to download Google Photos.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:58.010
It's a free app for Android and
iOS, and the cool thing with it

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:01.490
is that it's a really nice
intro to how our machine

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:03.320
learning works for imagery.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.800
You put it on your phone,
give it access to your photos,

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:09.260
it pushes them to the
cloud, performs the ML,

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:12.020
and then provides
that data back to you.

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:13.730
But you don't see
the outcome of it,

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:15.960
you just are able
to type in search.

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:18.646
You can type in dog and
you can get all the dogs

00:16:18.646 --> 00:16:19.520
and your photographs.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:21.680
But if you type in
Husky, Adrian's dog

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:23.960
will pop up on the
screen, as he has a husky.

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:26.960
So it's a nice way
to kind of go, well,

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:28.719
that's actually kind of cool.

00:16:28.719 --> 00:16:31.010
That'll give you an opportunity
to see what the API can

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:32.870
do in an application setting.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:35.100
The APIs are available
for anyone to use.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:37.520
You can also, in
concert with that,

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.200
work with your custom model.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.390
So in terms of that,
the normal process

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:44.780
is working with a
training workflow.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:48.530
And then we've announced
today, this morning

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:52.010
I believe, data prep which
provides you the opportunity

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:54.560
to be able to simplify
the machine learning

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:55.760
training process for you.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:17:01.580
As far as preparing your data
in a more accessible fashion

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:04.450
for you as a user to be
able to train your models.

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:06.650
And the prediction
flow is depicted below,

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:09.290
and that enables you to
be able to actually run

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:11.520
the model that you've trained.

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:13.770
So with that said, I'd like
to invite Adrian Graham up

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:18.880
on stage to talk with us
about simulation for media.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:20.356
Thanks, man.

00:17:20.356 --> 00:17:22.054
ADRIAN GRAHAM: Hey, everybody.

00:17:22.054 --> 00:17:22.970
Everybody can hear me?

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:23.470
Good.

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:26.564
Oh, I've got a clicker,
forward and back.

00:17:26.564 --> 00:17:27.730
So my name is Adrian Graham.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:30.860
I'm a cloud Solutions Architect
out of the Venice office,

00:17:30.860 --> 00:17:33.920
which is Venice, California,
not Venice, Italy.

00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:37.590
The food is pretty good,
at the office still.

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:40.582
So my background is I come
from the visual effects world.

00:17:40.582 --> 00:17:43.040
I spent 20 years working at
companies like Industrial Light

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:46.100
and Magic and Double
Negative in London,

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:48.560
and Digital Domain in
Venice, which is actually

00:17:48.560 --> 00:17:50.210
where the Google office is now.

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:55.370
But one of my focuses, foci,
if you will, was on simulation.

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:58.640
And simulation is a good
practical application

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:02.810
for how we deal with storage
on any platform, local

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:05.090
or on cloud.

00:18:05.090 --> 00:18:07.040
There's many challenges.

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:10.400
And if you think about what
a simulation artist who's

00:18:10.400 --> 00:18:13.070
going to do a fluid dynamics
simulation for example,

00:18:13.070 --> 00:18:17.150
they need a monster machine
with a ton of CPUs, maybe GPUs

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:18.784
if you've got GPU acceleration.

00:18:18.784 --> 00:18:21.200
Massive amounts of RAM, because
once you generate the data

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:23.570
you want to read it back
in and hold it in memory

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:25.565
so that you could
scrub back and forth.

00:18:25.565 --> 00:18:27.440
And obviously there's
a lot of disk required.

00:18:30.200 --> 00:18:33.320
The data that you're generating
is on a per frame basis

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:35.450
and you're generating
data for things

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.820
that has fluid simulation,
like the liquid and the foam

00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:39.466
and the spray and the mist.

00:18:39.466 --> 00:18:41.090
And all those are on
a per frame basis,

00:18:41.090 --> 00:18:43.850
and there could be thousands,
tens of thousands, millions,

00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:46.280
or hundreds of
millions of elements.

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:48.695
So what do you do
with all this data?

00:18:48.695 --> 00:18:49.570
How do you manage it?

00:18:49.570 --> 00:18:54.620
And in that realm, we're only
talking about a production

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:56.750
problem, where you've
got multiple iterations

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:59.400
of the same effect.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:01.800
And I'm going to use this
story from my old days

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:02.940
back at Digital Domain.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:05.640
We worked in this production
on this commercial

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:08.912
for a video game
called The Darkness.

00:19:08.912 --> 00:19:10.620
I can't speak to the
quality of the game,

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:16.560
but we had to create these very
sinewy, fluidy, smoky elements

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:18.480
that this character
could control.

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:20.340
And we use a tool
called Krakatoa.

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.340
Krakatoa is a way,
it's almost a pipeline

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:26.280
for generating simulation.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:29.380
And the workflow is such that
you generate one simulation,

00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:33.480
and then you run that simulation
hundreds or thousands of times,

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:35.550
each with a
different random seed

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:39.330
to generate slight,
slight variations.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:42.570
And then you could take all
those sims as a single element,

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:45.140
and then you can combine
them and render them as one.

00:19:45.140 --> 00:19:48.570
And that allows you to create
this long sinewy, smokey

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:49.724
effect.

00:19:49.724 --> 00:19:51.390
Now that's just one
element, these shots

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:55.080
might have 8, 10, 20,
30 elements in them.

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:57.580
And they're written
on a per frame basis.

00:19:57.580 --> 00:20:00.240
So when you think about
you needed thousands

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:05.310
of passes for each element,
each a shot might be 240 frames.

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:07.740
Remembering we don't
render to movie files,

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:09.480
we render on a per frame basis.

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:12.540
A 10 second shot is 240 frames.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:17.360
So when you add all that up,
my example at the bottom here,

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:21.650
the end of the story is that
I got a visit from the Senior

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:23.420
System Admin telling
me that I was using

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:28.390
exactly 43% of all
storage at the facility,

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:31.410
and this is one
take of one effect.

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:37.280
So that is a huge problem, which
leads me to the next slide.

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:39.074
Because when we
only have one take,

00:20:39.074 --> 00:20:41.240
what do we do if we want
to generate multiple takes?

00:20:41.240 --> 00:20:43.250
What if the supervisor
says, that's great

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:45.545
but make it blow up
a little more here

00:20:45.545 --> 00:20:47.570
or make it move a little faster.

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:49.730
And instead of just
running one simulation,

00:20:49.730 --> 00:20:52.850
you want to run a whole bunch of
them, each with tiny variations

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:55.617
so that the supervisor can
look and say, I like that one

00:20:55.617 --> 00:20:56.450
and I like that one.

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:59.650
Go in the middle of that.

00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:02.920
A good example here is the
wedge of this fluid dynamics

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:03.650
simulation.

00:21:03.650 --> 00:21:06.370
I'm only changing one
attribute, and that's

00:21:06.370 --> 00:21:08.350
this max Voxel size,
which is essentially

00:21:08.350 --> 00:21:10.540
the resolution of
the simulation.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:15.660
But you could see tweaking one
tiny little value exponentially

00:21:15.660 --> 00:21:21.630
changes the outcome, 25
million voxels from 226,000

00:21:21.630 --> 00:21:23.590
from changing one value.

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:27.600
So what this means is what
if we could run all three

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:32.070
at the same time, and then
view them at the same time

00:21:32.070 --> 00:21:33.210
and be able to choose?

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:36.060
And this is just one attribute,
you need to be able to--

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:38.560
these numbers are not
necessarily all normalized.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:40.320
So you've got all
different attributes

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:42.734
that require all different
kinds of inputs and ideas

00:21:42.734 --> 00:21:43.650
as to how to run them.

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:50.470
So put it on the cloud.

00:21:50.470 --> 00:21:53.260
So let's talk about
how, real quick,

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:54.610
how I did that simulation.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:56.711
That was a real quick
and dirty simulation

00:21:56.711 --> 00:21:58.210
that you saw a
couple of slides ago.

00:21:58.210 --> 00:22:04.120
I did it two days ago within a
couple of hours on the cloud.

00:22:04.120 --> 00:22:08.230
But if we want to get a little
bit technical here, if I may,

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:11.230
we've got a couple scripts
that we're running.

00:22:11.230 --> 00:22:14.260
We're running script
that runs inside of Maya.

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:17.050
We're using Maya bifrost, which
is a fluid dynamics simulation

00:22:17.050 --> 00:22:20.770
engine inside of a product
called Maya by Autodesk.

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:22.814
It's got a proprietary
scripting format called

00:22:22.814 --> 00:22:23.980
MEL, which is very sea-like.

00:22:23.980 --> 00:22:24.290
Like

00:22:24.290 --> 00:22:25.780
There's a tiny
little snippet here.

00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:29.590
All it's doing is saying list
over all the fluid containers,

00:22:29.590 --> 00:22:33.910
and for each container write out
the data that it's generating.

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:37.120
That, in turn, is controlled
by a Python script

00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:39.859
which takes in-- that's your
UI, that's your front end.

00:22:39.859 --> 00:22:42.400
And this is all command line,
we're not doing anything fancy.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:45.310
And I'll show you what
the architectural diagram

00:22:45.310 --> 00:22:46.490
for this looks like.

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:49.210
But essentially, it's taking
a start frame and an end frame

00:22:49.210 --> 00:22:54.200
and a variable value and an
out-directory, and that's it.

00:22:54.200 --> 00:22:57.760
So if we look at what the
command line, an example of two

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:00.850
wedging command lines
might be, in this case

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:05.209
we've got max Voxel size
or MVS of 0.05 and 0.001.

00:23:05.209 --> 00:23:07.000
and I would run these
two separate commands

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:09.130
on two separate VMs.

00:23:09.130 --> 00:23:11.710
So I can spin up a
managed instance group

00:23:11.710 --> 00:23:13.580
with as many as I want.

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:17.110
And I could deploy
one sim to each group,

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:19.390
or sorry, one sim to
each VM in the group.

00:23:24.200 --> 00:23:26.390
What do we do with
all this data?

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:29.640
Hope everybody gets the
Seinfeld reference there.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:32.120
So once we've got
our chosen affect

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:34.700
and we've got the
magic numbers and we've

00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:37.760
got an idea of exactly
where we want to go,

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:39.950
the output is going to
be the base simulation.

00:23:39.950 --> 00:23:43.130
So we run it at full resolution
of whatever settings we want,

00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:45.620
but we're not done,
we've got to mesh that.

00:23:45.620 --> 00:23:47.270
And meshing is a
whole separate--

00:23:47.270 --> 00:23:49.100
it's not a simulation
but it's a procedure

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:53.290
that runs on the simulation
data to surface it.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:55.990
And from there we might want
to generate foam and mist

00:23:55.990 --> 00:23:58.630
and spray and bubbles.

00:23:58.630 --> 00:24:01.150
And those are all
separate simulations,

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:03.640
and all those simulations
generate data.

00:24:03.640 --> 00:24:05.300
And how does it generate data?

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:09.970
It rams every CPU on
the box and spits out

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:12.350
massive, massive
amounts of data.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:14.560
Which is ideal for
local SSD, for example,

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:16.750
up to three terabytes
in storage, what is it,

00:24:16.750 --> 00:24:18.010
700,000 IOPS.

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:20.860
It will absorb that
data in the blink

00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:23.260
of an eye with no problem,
and you won't get a visit

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:25.467
from your local system admin.

00:24:25.467 --> 00:24:27.550
And then once you're done
with all the simulation,

00:24:27.550 --> 00:24:28.960
you want to perform rendering.

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:30.730
And that is actually
the subject of a talk

00:24:30.730 --> 00:24:34.510
that I gave a couple of hours
ago on rendering in the cloud

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:39.130
and running a remote
display over Teradici,

00:24:39.130 --> 00:24:40.970
which I'll talk
about in a second.

00:24:40.970 --> 00:24:46.590
So, quite simply, the set up
that I used to run all this

00:24:46.590 --> 00:24:48.130
is extremely simple.

00:24:48.130 --> 00:24:51.584
I just have one, I only
had one VM in the cloud,

00:24:51.584 --> 00:24:53.250
but I'm using my local
workstation which

00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:55.590
is just my Mac running Maya.

00:24:55.590 --> 00:25:01.410
And I saved the file, I used
GSU to copy the files over,

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:04.276
and I just ran a shell
on the remote machine.

00:25:04.276 --> 00:25:05.650
I actually have
a license server.

00:25:05.650 --> 00:25:07.530
That's another problem, you've
got to deal with licenses.

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:09.300
But you need to
license the software.

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:11.400
So I'm running a license
server on the cloud

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:15.720
with feeding a license to
the remote display, or sorry,

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:18.840
to the remote machine as
well as to my own machine.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:20.790
This is what Jeff
showed earlier.

00:25:20.790 --> 00:25:24.270
It's a little more
legible, but it's

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:27.810
an extremely detailed layout
of how an entire visual effects

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:29.352
pipeline might be
built in the cloud.

00:25:29.352 --> 00:25:31.101
If you want to see
more of this, or if you

00:25:31.101 --> 00:25:33.760
want to talk about this, come
see us at the fridge texture

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:36.894
downstairs and we can
actually lay this out

00:25:36.894 --> 00:25:39.060
and talk about it using
these little fridge magnets.

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:42.000
I hope everybody's seen it,
it's really a lot of fun.

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:47.260
Now, what I showed earlier
and I think it was recorded,

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:51.570
so you can watch it later, is
I did a remote desktop session

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:52.260
into the cloud.

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:55.790
So I didn't even have local
capabilities, I was using--

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:58.440
I was going over
Teradici PC over IP,

00:25:58.440 --> 00:26:01.380
which is protocol
for remote desktop

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:02.760
by a company called Teradici.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:07.370
And I'm running, in
this case, a Windows box

00:26:07.370 --> 00:26:12.750
in the US central data center.

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:14.300
Which is about 1,800 miles away.

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:17.019
And the ping time was about
40 milliseconds round.

00:26:17.019 --> 00:26:19.560
Pretty amazing what's going on
here because we were rendering

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:24.340
on 128 GPUs, 128 KAEs.

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:28.030
So, in all, thinking
about the powerhouse

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:30.070
that we have available
to us in the cloud

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:33.970
or how we could build a power
house, a 64 core machine,

00:26:33.970 --> 00:26:37.130
nobody is going to
buy you one of those

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:39.370
if you work in visual effects.

00:26:39.370 --> 00:26:42.430
But the fact that we can have
almost a half a terabyte of RAM

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:44.330
in the visual effects
world is unbelievable.

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:46.587
And at $0.80 an hour,
you can't beat that.

00:26:46.587 --> 00:26:48.670
And you can of course
attach up to three terabytes

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:51.970
of local SSD, or PDSSD.

00:26:51.970 --> 00:26:56.304
Compute GPUs, there's
also a number of renderers

00:26:56.304 --> 00:26:57.470
that are running on the GPU.

00:26:57.470 --> 00:27:00.430
There's Redshift and V-ray
RT and that type of stuff.

00:27:00.430 --> 00:27:02.200
And the throughput
is pretty amazing,

00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:07.910
up, to 16 gigabits per
second within a zone.

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:11.500
This demo, I urge you to go look
at it once it's posted online,

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:13.720
is that we've got
this massive seam that

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:18.340
is rendering on those 128
GPUs, and on my local Windows

00:27:18.340 --> 00:27:20.530
remote instance, not
local, but remote instance.

00:27:20.530 --> 00:27:22.960
I'm tumbling in almost real
time and it's resolving

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:26.000
in less than five seconds.

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:29.650
So, with that, we're going
to hand, the aluminum pole

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:31.420
over to Mike Warren.

00:27:35.488 --> 00:27:37.726
Let me unlock it for you.

00:27:37.726 --> 00:27:38.850
MIKE WARREN: Thanks Adrian.

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:44.446
I wanted to start with a
little bit about myself.

00:27:44.446 --> 00:27:48.550
I have a PhD in
astrophysics, and my PhD

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:52.510
was simulating the universe,
the dark matter in the universe,

00:27:52.510 --> 00:27:56.320
using initially a
million particles.

00:27:56.320 --> 00:28:01.300
So over the span of my
graduate school career

00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:03.744
to about three years
ago those simulations

00:28:03.744 --> 00:28:06.160
with the same software went
from about a million particles

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:07.870
to a trillion particles.

00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:10.990
So about a factor of
a million in scale,

00:28:10.990 --> 00:28:14.380
and about that same factor
in computing performance.

00:28:14.380 --> 00:28:17.960
And that came from Moore's
law combined with the scaling

00:28:17.960 --> 00:28:21.220
in parallel machines.

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:24.610
And kind of in
the midst of that,

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:27.460
the popularity of parallel
machines got to the point

00:28:27.460 --> 00:28:30.790
where we couldn't get time
on our local supercomputer

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:31.370
anymore.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:33.130
So we started building
our own machines.

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:37.780
So you see me there on
the cover of Linux Journal

00:28:37.780 --> 00:28:42.640
with a cluster of 16 PCs running
Intel processors connected

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:46.660
with ethernet, a very
normal architecture

00:28:46.660 --> 00:28:50.480
these days but this
is 20 years ago.

00:28:50.480 --> 00:28:54.010
So we've been working in this
same sort of environment that

00:28:54.010 --> 00:28:57.640
would be completely familiar to
someone logging in the command

00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:03.170
line of a Google Compute node.

00:29:03.170 --> 00:29:07.340
And we've kind been living
in that world for the last 20

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:08.040
years or so.

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:16.790
So Descartes Labs was
founded just a little

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:21.260
over two years ago, some
scientists from Los Alamos.

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:26.600
We've doubled in size twice
now to almost 30 people.

00:29:26.600 --> 00:29:30.290
We've now got an office here in
San Francisco and another one

00:29:30.290 --> 00:29:31.250
in New York City.

00:29:33.910 --> 00:29:37.660
And I want to talk about
today one of the problems

00:29:37.660 --> 00:29:39.820
that we run into
at Descartes labs

00:29:39.820 --> 00:29:43.680
and what we did to solve it.

00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:47.820
And then at the end, I
want to do a demo of one

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:50.100
of our products that
we just released

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:53.580
earlier this week on Tuesday
which we call GeoVisual Search.

00:29:57.630 --> 00:30:04.290
So one of the key reasons we
thought Descartes Labs was

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:07.350
a good fit as a startup,
is that satellites

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:10.830
produce a lot of data.

00:30:10.830 --> 00:30:12.952
This is an imagery
made out of Landsat

00:30:12.952 --> 00:30:18.510
8 data, kind of an
endless summer day

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:19.920
on the surface of the Earth.

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:22.020
The earth has never
looked like this,

00:30:22.020 --> 00:30:24.930
it requires quite
a lot of processing

00:30:24.930 --> 00:30:29.040
to get rid of all the clouds
and artifacts and other things.

00:30:29.040 --> 00:30:32.040
But it makes a nice
picture to look at.

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:34.200
But you can't really
appreciate from this image

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:36.330
the fact that there's,
underneath this,

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:41.490
about 320 terabytes of data that
had to be processed in order

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:44.340
to make this image
that you can zoom

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:50.640
into any part of by
a factor of 1,000.

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:54.660
So I was going to use a quote
from Ken Batcher here about

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:57.920
I/O-bound problems, but the
lawyer said that would violate

00:30:57.920 --> 00:30:58.670
copyright.

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:04.000
So I'm quoting myself
from a few years ago,

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:07.100
"A supercomputer is a device
for storing obsolete software

00:31:07.100 --> 00:31:08.742
libraries."

00:31:08.742 --> 00:31:10.700
And this is kind of to
point out the difference

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:14.720
between traditional
HPC and the cloud.

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:18.020
And you run something
on an HPC machine

00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:21.760
those libraries just
stick around forever.

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:27.910
The Fortran matrix multiplies
that seem to never die

00:31:27.910 --> 00:31:30.260
are going to be on every
one of those machines.

00:31:30.260 --> 00:31:36.050
And at Descartes we started
fresh knowing that the cloud

00:31:36.050 --> 00:31:39.150
was going to be the future.

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:43.310
And you can see that, today,
the fact that Intel came first

00:31:43.310 --> 00:31:47.270
to Google with Skylake, not
the national laboratories

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:50.876
that had traditionally been
the leaders in supercomputing.

00:31:54.070 --> 00:31:58.540
So what was the
problem we ran into?

00:31:58.540 --> 00:32:02.830
And it's a core part
of the Unix philosophy

00:32:02.830 --> 00:32:06.410
that everything is a file.

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:11.170
So the cloud does a lot of
things that are really helpful,

00:32:11.170 --> 00:32:13.690
but one of the
things it doesn't do

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:17.740
is make architecting and
developing application software

00:32:17.740 --> 00:32:19.660
easier.

00:32:19.660 --> 00:32:28.600
So object storage, anything you
stick in GS:// does not conform

00:32:28.600 --> 00:32:33.550
to the everything is a file
interface and it breaks a great

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:36.000
many things.

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:44.060
So the traditional POSIX
interface to a file is open,

00:32:44.060 --> 00:32:48.470
read, seek, right, close.

00:32:48.470 --> 00:32:54.850
You can't do any of those things
on an object in Google Storage.

00:32:54.850 --> 00:32:58.660
You, instead, have a RESTful
interface through HTTP

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:00.850
doing GET and PUT.

00:33:00.850 --> 00:33:05.770
And you don't have a file system
hierarchy with directories,

00:33:05.770 --> 00:33:10.810
each object is labeled with
a globally unique identifier.

00:33:10.810 --> 00:33:12.640
So one of the things
you can do with a file

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:15.070
is update a block
inside that file

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:20.590
without changing the rest of
the data, but in an object store

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:23.470
if you change one
bit in that object

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:27.370
rewrite the entire object.

00:33:27.370 --> 00:33:29.770
Those constraints are
there for a reason,

00:33:29.770 --> 00:33:33.820
it makes object storage much
more scalable and much less

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:36.120
expensive.

00:33:36.120 --> 00:33:37.730
But the cost that
you do have to pay

00:33:37.730 --> 00:33:40.970
is modifying code
that already exists

00:33:40.970 --> 00:33:43.700
to work with an object store
rather than a file system.

00:33:48.410 --> 00:33:51.770
So to give you an idea of
the relative importance

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:55.040
of these different
interfaces, we

00:33:55.040 --> 00:34:00.980
used Google BigQuery with the
public GetHub repo database

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:04.490
and ran this sequel
query it to show

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:07.910
how often these various
interfaces show up

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:10.639
in the code on GetHub.

00:34:10.639 --> 00:34:16.280
So f open showed up
about 740,000 times.

00:34:16.280 --> 00:34:20.000
All of the rest of these
interfaces are less than 10%

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:20.900
of that.

00:34:20.900 --> 00:34:24.290
So 90% of the code
out there is going

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:28.680
to be using a POSIX interface
and expect a file system.

00:34:32.810 --> 00:34:37.120
So how do you match
these two API together?

00:34:37.120 --> 00:34:40.570
Well, the typical approach
is you copy things

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:46.540
from object storage to a local
file using the persistent disks

00:34:46.540 --> 00:34:49.150
that Jeff talked about.

00:34:49.150 --> 00:34:52.159
So if you've got
relatively small files,

00:34:52.159 --> 00:34:56.949
making a copy in the local
storage works pretty well.

00:34:56.949 --> 00:35:00.910
You do a GSU held copy, it shows
up in your local file system,

00:35:00.910 --> 00:35:04.580
and then your application
can read that data in.

00:35:04.580 --> 00:35:06.670
And that second read
is actually very fast

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:11.680
because that data is coming from
memory, because the operating

00:35:11.680 --> 00:35:16.090
system is clever and caches
all of that data in the buffer

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:18.820
cache.

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:24.820
But when we get to larger
files and higher data rates,

00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:27.430
we're going to run
into some restrictions.

00:35:27.430 --> 00:35:31.480
Because a PD hard
drive has a limit

00:35:31.480 --> 00:35:33.940
of about 180
megabytes per second

00:35:33.940 --> 00:35:35.920
of read bandwidth
and 120 megabytes

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:38.470
a second of write bandwidth.

00:35:38.470 --> 00:35:41.690
The object store can actually
provide data much faster

00:35:41.690 --> 00:35:44.720
than that, but you can't
ingest it and write it

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:47.390
to the disk at those rates.

00:35:47.390 --> 00:35:49.820
Although, if you
paid attention today

00:35:49.820 --> 00:35:55.370
Google announced with SSDs
you can now configure machines

00:35:55.370 --> 00:36:00.290
with a PDSSD with something
like 800 megabytes

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:02.570
of read bandwidth on your node.

00:36:05.590 --> 00:36:08.590
And what is often
the ideal situation,

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:13.270
if your files can fit
in less than about

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:17.530
half the memory of the machine
you just set up a RAM disk

00:36:17.530 --> 00:36:20.890
and read the files from object
storage into that RAM disk.

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:24.760
It never runs into any of
these restrictions, and in fact

00:36:24.760 --> 00:36:26.980
you then don't need
a local disk at all.

00:36:32.060 --> 00:36:33.980
You can also try to
rewrite your code

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:37.160
and not deal with a
file system at all.

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:41.720
Go directly from the
object store into memory.

00:36:41.720 --> 00:36:45.800
We like to do this with
Python where you can actually

00:36:45.800 --> 00:36:50.270
do a GSU to a CAT on the
object through a pipe,

00:36:50.270 --> 00:36:52.640
and then Python can
redirect that pipe

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:55.370
into a memory buffer
which you can pass off

00:36:55.370 --> 00:36:58.780
to your application code.

00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:02.260
But one of the
downsides of this is now

00:37:02.260 --> 00:37:05.290
that data is in your
application memory

00:37:05.290 --> 00:37:07.930
and it can't be shared
by other processes that

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:09.880
may be running on that machine.

00:37:09.880 --> 00:37:13.870
And that's very much unlike
the file system, which

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:17.380
can share data to multiple
processes out of the buffer

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:19.530
cache.

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:23.130
And it's actually fairly hard
to write interfaces like that

00:37:23.130 --> 00:37:25.140
because a lot of
application codes

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:28.200
are going to want a file
pointer or a file name

00:37:28.200 --> 00:37:30.945
and you have to dig down
into the guts of the code

00:37:30.945 --> 00:37:35.460
to actually find the memory
buffer that you want access to.

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:43.000
So, finally, object storage
just isn't an option

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:47.770
if you've got very large files,
hundreds of gigabytes which

00:37:47.770 --> 00:37:50.020
are bigger than the
memory of the machine

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:52.990
that you need random access to.

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:57.350
There's nothing
you can do, apart

00:37:57.350 --> 00:38:01.070
from the former solutions,
which are often, very

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:03.530
slow of reading to local disk.

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:07.190
So what you really
want to do is actually

00:38:07.190 --> 00:38:12.350
put a virtual file system
on top of the object store.

00:38:12.350 --> 00:38:15.350
And the Linux
operating system has

00:38:15.350 --> 00:38:19.700
supported that for a long time
through the virtual file system

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:20.375
they call FUSE.

00:38:23.430 --> 00:38:27.650
And here you can see the
first command in Festivus

00:38:27.650 --> 00:38:31.040
was January 22, 2016.

00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:35.510
So it's just a bit
over a year old.

00:38:35.510 --> 00:38:37.917
But why did we write
this code ourselves?

00:38:37.917 --> 00:38:39.500
Isn't this an important
enough problem

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:42.290
that there are lots of
open source solutions?

00:38:45.750 --> 00:38:49.410
So we tried all the
solutions we could find.

00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:56.040
There's gcsfuse, s3fs, a number
of others, but none of them

00:38:56.040 --> 00:39:00.120
performed at the level
that we required.

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:04.680
And the reasons were they
were just slow at reading,

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:08.550
and they handled the
metadata very slowly

00:39:08.550 --> 00:39:12.600
in terms of figuring out
what files were, what

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:14.410
their modification dates were.

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:20.040
So what is Festivus?

00:39:23.160 --> 00:39:28.590
It uses the asynchronous API
of libfuse in the Linux kernel.

00:39:32.090 --> 00:39:34.610
The kernel does callbacks
to this userspace code.

00:39:34.610 --> 00:39:39.710
When you do a system call
like open in the kernel

00:39:39.710 --> 00:39:44.090
it knows to talk
to the fuse module

00:39:44.090 --> 00:39:49.520
calling the open stub
inside of that code

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:55.490
and doing the right thing
over HTTP to the object store.

00:39:55.490 --> 00:39:58.970
and rather than talk to the
object store for metadata,

00:39:58.970 --> 00:40:02.000
which is often
very slow, we just

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:07.020
put all of that in a separate
in memory key value store

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:09.770
so we can access that
with very low latency.

00:40:12.710 --> 00:40:15.670
So a very important
optimization in all of this

00:40:15.670 --> 00:40:19.480
is actually modifying
the Linux kernel.

00:40:19.480 --> 00:40:21.500
Some of these
parameters in the kernel

00:40:21.500 --> 00:40:25.460
have been set specifically
with disks in mind

00:40:25.460 --> 00:40:29.870
where a block size
is four kilobytes

00:40:29.870 --> 00:40:34.970
and latency is typically
below 10 milliseconds.

00:40:34.970 --> 00:40:37.590
And now we're talking
over the network,

00:40:37.590 --> 00:40:42.620
and we can read four megabytes
in about the same time

00:40:42.620 --> 00:40:46.880
as it takes to read
four kilobytes.

00:40:46.880 --> 00:40:51.650
So we want to increase
the kernel parameter

00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:54.080
for the maximum number
of pages per request

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:59.720
from the default of 32, which
limits the size of the read

00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:03.740
request you can make to the
object store of 128 kilobytes

00:41:03.740 --> 00:41:04.920
to a much larger value.

00:41:04.920 --> 00:41:09.860
So you can actually do
requests at 4 megabytes.

00:41:09.860 --> 00:41:13.070
So since that requires
a kernel update,

00:41:13.070 --> 00:41:16.574
we have to deploy all our
systems with a modified kernel.

00:41:16.574 --> 00:41:17.990
And if you are
somewhere where you

00:41:17.990 --> 00:41:21.440
don't have control
over the kernel,

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:22.940
you won't be able to do this.

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:26.390
If you're working in
Kubernetes and the kernel

00:41:26.390 --> 00:41:30.530
hasn't been configured
with this parameter,

00:41:30.530 --> 00:41:34.730
you can't access this
increased performance.

00:41:40.650 --> 00:41:43.620
So how does it measure up?

00:41:43.620 --> 00:41:48.060
So we can see the
first table we're

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:50.520
looking at the
bandwidth and gigabytes

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:55.470
per second on different node
types initially, and then

00:41:55.470 --> 00:41:59.550
different numbers of nodes
reading in parallel to the file

00:41:59.550 --> 00:42:00.810
system.

00:42:00.810 --> 00:42:05.280
So the bottom line
there is 231 gigabytes

00:42:05.280 --> 00:42:08.301
per second of I/O bandwidth.

00:42:11.390 --> 00:42:16.950
Now that's an impressive number
even at a Supercomputer Center

00:42:16.950 --> 00:42:19.170
where you've spent tens
of millions of dollars

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:22.980
on your Luster file system.

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:24.440
In fact, you've
spent so much money

00:42:24.440 --> 00:42:27.690
on that file system you don't
let people store files there

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:32.050
persistently, it's
mostly a scratch disk

00:42:32.050 --> 00:42:35.900
that has a fixed time.

00:42:35.900 --> 00:42:39.740
But this bandwidth is going to
normal, multi-region, Google

00:42:39.740 --> 00:42:40.670
Storage.

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:48.320
So it's reliable, it's
scalable, it's persistent.

00:42:48.320 --> 00:42:51.285
So it's exactly what you want
for a high performance file

00:42:51.285 --> 00:42:51.785
system.

00:42:55.940 --> 00:42:59.750
The other table
shows the performance

00:42:59.750 --> 00:43:05.347
versus gcsfuse, which is an open
source implementation, of what

00:43:05.347 --> 00:43:07.430
sort of performance you
get if you're doing random

00:43:07.430 --> 00:43:14.420
reads in a file, where you
can't depend on prefetching

00:43:14.420 --> 00:43:18.780
to give you better performance.

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:21.110
So you can see that if you
typically read something

00:43:21.110 --> 00:43:26.000
like 1 megabyte blocks,
Festivus is giving you

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:31.670
270 megabytes per second
versus gcsfuse is down more

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:34.890
than a factor of 20 from that.

00:43:34.890 --> 00:43:38.040
And for very large
blocks we're basically

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:45.180
up at the network limit of the
node at almost 1,600 megabytes

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:45.680
a second.

00:43:50.930 --> 00:43:56.730
So a question for you is
what's next for Festivus?

00:43:56.730 --> 00:44:00.180
It does what we want it
to do at Descartes labs,

00:44:00.180 --> 00:44:05.200
but is it useful
beyond our application?

00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:08.170
And we're a startup,
we didn't intend

00:44:08.170 --> 00:44:12.410
to write a file system
for other people to use.

00:44:12.410 --> 00:44:16.240
We've got other things
that keep us very busy.

00:44:16.240 --> 00:44:21.220
So what we have done, we
published a paper about this.

00:44:21.220 --> 00:44:23.320
So if you're motivated
you ought to be

00:44:23.320 --> 00:44:26.980
able to follow along
and implement something

00:44:26.980 --> 00:44:28.465
similar for your own use case.

00:44:31.090 --> 00:44:34.420
Could this be a viable
commercial product?

00:44:34.420 --> 00:44:38.440
I don't know what the
market is for a fused file

00:44:38.440 --> 00:44:43.380
system on top of object store.

00:44:43.380 --> 00:44:46.680
Would we be better off
just open sourcing it

00:44:46.680 --> 00:44:50.190
and letting the community
take over and take it

00:44:50.190 --> 00:44:51.190
wherever they will?

00:44:54.210 --> 00:44:58.170
And if you're not really
sensitive to performance,

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:01.250
you can use one of the other
open source implementations.

00:45:01.250 --> 00:45:04.335
It does the same things,
just not as quickly.

00:45:08.790 --> 00:45:11.990
So now for the
Festivus Miracles,

00:45:11.990 --> 00:45:15.380
what does this file
system enable us to do?

00:45:18.950 --> 00:45:23.430
And I'll go through a couple of
slides here kind of describing

00:45:23.430 --> 00:45:25.240
the demo I'm going to show you.

00:45:25.240 --> 00:45:27.520
But if you like you
can follow along.

00:45:27.520 --> 00:45:33.141
These are publicly released
maps at maps.descarteslabs.com,

00:45:33.141 --> 00:45:37.600
and our GeoVisual Search at
search.descarteslabs.com.

00:45:37.600 --> 00:45:41.360
So first maps.

00:45:44.650 --> 00:45:49.920
Now, everyone's used to
Google Map's earth layer

00:45:49.920 --> 00:45:54.790
where you can scroll around and
zoom in and out on the world.

00:45:54.790 --> 00:45:59.460
And we've done something
similar in the visual bands

00:45:59.460 --> 00:46:03.780
with Landsat 8, which doesn't
go to as high resolution,

00:46:03.780 --> 00:46:07.810
it is limited to 15
meters per pixel.

00:46:07.810 --> 00:46:11.820
And this was collected from
about four years of data

00:46:11.820 --> 00:46:15.260
from the Landsat 8 satellite.

00:46:15.260 --> 00:46:18.380
But beyond the
normal visual bands,

00:46:18.380 --> 00:46:23.540
there are some newer satellites
that collect information

00:46:23.540 --> 00:46:25.770
in very different ways.

00:46:25.770 --> 00:46:29.330
So in the middle there
you see Sentinel-1, which

00:46:29.330 --> 00:46:32.900
uses synthetic aperture radar.

00:46:32.900 --> 00:46:37.160
So it shoots down microwaves
at the Earth and measures

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:39.330
what gets reflected back.

00:46:39.330 --> 00:46:42.470
So it can see through
clouds, and it

00:46:42.470 --> 00:46:47.270
can detect things that are
very difficult to detect

00:46:47.270 --> 00:46:48.560
in normal light.

00:46:51.640 --> 00:46:55.300
Finally, Sentinel-2 is the
European Space Agency's

00:46:55.300 --> 00:46:59.200
satellite, which also
has normal visual bands,

00:46:59.200 --> 00:47:03.790
but in addition has a set
of bands that are just

00:47:03.790 --> 00:47:06.820
beyond the visual into
the near infrared, which

00:47:06.820 --> 00:47:11.260
are very sensitive
at classifying

00:47:11.260 --> 00:47:13.270
different types of plants.

00:47:13.270 --> 00:47:20.620
So that's in the region where
the chlorophyll or the cells

00:47:20.620 --> 00:47:24.060
and the leaves of the plants
become very reflective

00:47:24.060 --> 00:47:27.170
in a near-infrared.

00:47:27.170 --> 00:47:29.810
So you're not just
limited to a normal map,

00:47:29.810 --> 00:47:31.760
you can look across
all of these bands.

00:47:35.230 --> 00:47:38.440
And this is just an example
of what the synthetic aperture

00:47:38.440 --> 00:47:43.240
radar looks like in this area,
where the bright yellow shows

00:47:43.240 --> 00:47:49.510
strong reflections,
often vertical buildings.

00:47:49.510 --> 00:47:52.210
If you look carefully
a little bit

00:47:52.210 --> 00:47:54.160
to the east of San
Francisco, there you

00:47:54.160 --> 00:48:00.430
can see some spots in the
water and those are actually

00:48:00.430 --> 00:48:04.960
where ships tend to anchor
themselves and have created

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:08.920
enough of a signal over the
average of all these images

00:48:08.920 --> 00:48:12.430
that were put together to
show that pattern of where

00:48:12.430 --> 00:48:13.870
the ships are anchored.

00:48:17.640 --> 00:48:21.000
And then the demo
I'll show you now

00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:26.640
is called GeoVisual Search,
where we divide Earth's surface

00:48:26.640 --> 00:48:30.851
into billions of tiny images
and we extract a visual feature

00:48:30.851 --> 00:48:31.350
vector.

00:48:31.350 --> 00:48:35.040
And this was done
using Google Cloud,

00:48:35.040 --> 00:48:38.350
using TensorFlow
on Google Cloud.

00:48:38.350 --> 00:48:41.460
And once we have
those feature vectors

00:48:41.460 --> 00:48:43.110
we then need a
way to search them

00:48:43.110 --> 00:48:45.990
very quickly so that
when someone clicks

00:48:45.990 --> 00:48:48.780
on a particular
type of object we

00:48:48.780 --> 00:48:51.390
can search through all
these billion images

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:55.860
for the 1,000 images that
looked the most like that.

00:48:55.860 --> 00:48:58.080
So if we can switch
over to the demo.

00:49:12.020 --> 00:49:16.630
So this is our map
of the United States.

00:49:16.630 --> 00:49:20.320
We can zoom in and out.

00:49:20.320 --> 00:49:28.510
We've done some sort
of preset searches.

00:49:28.510 --> 00:49:30.050
This was a waste
treatment plant,

00:49:30.050 --> 00:49:34.040
which is just south of
Los Angeles airport.

00:49:34.040 --> 00:49:36.040
And I'm not going
to use that one.

00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:38.950
I'm going to go up
here and you can

00:49:38.950 --> 00:49:46.775
see this is one of the
big parking lots near LAX.

00:49:49.860 --> 00:49:51.600
So I'm going to click that.

00:49:51.600 --> 00:49:57.440
And in that amount of time it's
found the 402 closest matches

00:49:57.440 --> 00:50:00.690
across the United States,
and showing you up

00:50:00.690 --> 00:50:04.040
on this map where they are.

00:50:04.040 --> 00:50:09.620
And down here we see the
image chips from each of those

00:50:09.620 --> 00:50:10.460
matches.

00:50:10.460 --> 00:50:13.670
And you can see it's
done a very good job

00:50:13.670 --> 00:50:18.040
in finding those parking lots.

00:50:18.040 --> 00:50:22.510
So we can go up here and check
some of these other points

00:50:22.510 --> 00:50:25.450
to see are they
really parking lots.

00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:35.070
There's one, that
was a parking lot.

00:50:38.542 --> 00:50:40.235
And go up here to North Dakota.

00:50:46.090 --> 00:50:50.740
Turns out this is not
actually a parking lot,

00:50:50.740 --> 00:50:59.310
but it's a junkyard of cars
parked next to each other.

00:50:59.310 --> 00:51:03.930
So that's just pointing out
that it's GeoVisual Search,

00:51:03.930 --> 00:51:06.390
it's things that look similar.

00:51:06.390 --> 00:51:08.760
And you would need
other layers to go

00:51:08.760 --> 00:51:14.115
beyond that for very
sophisticated searches.

00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:19.935
We can do another one here
just of these wind turbines.

00:51:22.790 --> 00:51:25.749
So there you can see
these kind of cluster

00:51:25.749 --> 00:51:27.665
in the middle of the
country where it's windy.

00:51:32.980 --> 00:51:37.130
But, again, we can go
and check our accuracy.

00:51:39.784 --> 00:51:41.075
Here's some more wind turbines.

00:51:44.270 --> 00:51:46.990
So if you'd like
to play with that

00:51:46.990 --> 00:51:51.280
yourself just go to
search.decarteslabs.com.

00:51:51.280 --> 00:51:54.679
Reserve yourself some time,
because it's fairly addictive.

00:52:02.830 --> 00:52:05.560
So to wrap up, I wanted
to talk a little bit

00:52:05.560 --> 00:52:09.100
about the Descartes
lab's platform.

00:52:09.100 --> 00:52:16.060
So what we've done is taken data
from all the public satellites,

00:52:16.060 --> 00:52:18.080
we're a partner with Planet.

00:52:18.080 --> 00:52:21.160
Will Kirkpatrick is
going to be giving

00:52:21.160 --> 00:52:24.280
part of the keynote
tomorrow morning.

00:52:24.280 --> 00:52:26.500
Today, they
announced they'd move

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:29.320
seven petabytes of their
data into Google Cloud

00:52:29.320 --> 00:52:31.030
and are now going
to be distributing

00:52:31.030 --> 00:52:36.250
all of their imagery from GCP.

00:52:36.250 --> 00:52:38.350
So our platform
is intended to get

00:52:38.350 --> 00:52:40.780
rid of a lot of the
friction that people

00:52:40.780 --> 00:52:44.810
deal with with these
massive satellite data sets.

00:52:44.810 --> 00:52:48.650
So we have a very
simple Python API,

00:52:48.650 --> 00:52:51.970
which allows you to
query and retrieve

00:52:51.970 --> 00:52:57.520
raster imagery across
sensors spectral bands, time.

00:52:57.520 --> 00:53:02.530
You can leverage the whole
ecosystem of cloud tools

00:53:02.530 --> 00:53:04.840
on this imagery
however we would like.

00:53:04.840 --> 00:53:07.570
We leverage the cloud to
be able to scale jobs up

00:53:07.570 --> 00:53:10.910
to thousands of nodes.

00:53:10.910 --> 00:53:13.150
This is a closed beta now.

00:53:13.150 --> 00:53:16.150
If you're interested,
please contact us--

00:53:16.150 --> 00:53:18.791
hello@descarteslabs.com.

00:53:18.791 --> 00:53:23.970
Later this summer it
will become more open.

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:30.650
So finally if you'd
like to learn more

00:53:30.650 --> 00:53:34.410
about the paper I mentioned
earlier, the link is there.

00:53:34.410 --> 00:53:37.500
We have a technical
blog post on medium

00:53:37.500 --> 00:53:41.760
describing GeoVisual
Search on our home page

00:53:41.760 --> 00:53:43.865
descarteslabs.com.

00:53:43.865 --> 00:53:44.990
We're hiring.

00:53:44.990 --> 00:53:51.240
So if you love to work on
problems that are really

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:57.520
cutting edge and can help a
lot of people, let us know.

00:53:57.520 --> 00:53:58.020
Thank you.

00:54:03.817 --> 00:54:05.400
JEFF KEMBER: Really
want to thank Mike

00:54:05.400 --> 00:54:07.300
for being here today,
fantastic presentation.

00:54:07.300 --> 00:54:08.680
And Adrian, as well.

00:54:08.680 --> 00:54:09.180
Excellent.

00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:10.380
Thank you for coming.

00:54:10.380 --> 00:54:11.660
We're going to have a few
minutes for questions.

00:54:11.660 --> 00:54:13.140
There's microphones
in the center

00:54:13.140 --> 00:54:15.270
as well as on the
other side of the room.

00:54:15.270 --> 00:54:18.490
This is a great opportunity
to ask questions.

00:54:18.490 --> 00:54:22.670
Please come up and let us
